Systems, boundaries and thermal vocabulary (@th-vocabulary: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer, #source "../sources/Introduction-to-Engineering-Thermodynamics-Yan.lingua#r_6AX93SRMGE0XXJTQ1NW6X9QXTW", #source "../sources/Thermodynamics-and-Chemistry-DeVoe.lingua#r_2YHHGY26Y8X9MS9DB3MXZHD1D1", #source "../sources/Termodinamica-Lesche-UFJF.lingua#r_0ABCKSRZBN8T4Y4FV1FQPWNY4Z", #thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer "../../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/foundations/Units-and-dimensions.lingua#r_3A511KGNX59K2YHKT83NM38NE1") { r_438839TWPYKN6MTK1CCNG2JBCF
**Read:** [Introduction to Engineering Thermodynamics — Claire Yu Yan](../sources/Introduction-to-Engineering-Thermodynamics-Yan.lingua#r_6AX93SRMGE0XXJTQ1NW6X9QXTW), chapters 1–2; [Thermodynamics and Chemistry — Howard DeVoe](../sources/Thermodynamics-and-Chemistry-DeVoe.lingua#r_2YHHGY26Y8X9MS9DB3MXZHD1D1), chapters 1–2; [Termodinâmica — Bernhard Lesche, UFJF](../sources/Termodinamica-Lesche-UFJF.lingua#r_0ABCKSRZBN8T4Y4FV1FQPWNY4Z), the complete introductory chapter.

A closed system contains a fixed quantity of matter; an open control volume permits flow across its boundary. An isolated system exchanges neither energy nor matter with its surroundings. A boundary can be real or chosen for analysis, stationary or moving. Specify it before writing a balance.

Separate a property of a state from a transfer during a process. Internal energy U and entropy S are state quantities; heat Q and work W describe boundary transfers. An extensive quantity scales with the amount of a simple homogeneous system, while its mass-specific or molar version uses a declared basis. A path between two states is additional information.

English/Portuguese pairs include heat/calor, work/trabalho, internal energy/energia interna, enthalpy/entalpia, entropy/entropia, heat capacity/capacidade térmica, thermal conductivity/condutividade térmica and heat flux/densidade de fluxo de calor. State whether a symbol means total quantity, rate, per-area flux, mass-specific or molar quantity. The word “heat” should not silently replace temperature or a material's internal energy.

